{"conjecture": "Software accumulates an implicit dependency graph wherein untested coupling between modules grows faster than tested coupling, because local correctness verifies explicit contracts but not the transitive assumptions those contracts rely on. Each accepted change that is locally correct but globally uncoordinated permanently increases the expected blast radius of future changes by creating new implicit invariants the system now depends on but no one has modeled. Thus, long-running systems degrade not from bad changes, but from the compounding entropy of individually-invisible, structurally-untracked cross-module assumptions that make the cost of certifying any new change grow super-linearly with system age."}
